## A. ACE detection surface — what you must beat

ACE is a multi-layer system; assume all of these run simultaneously.

| Layer | Mechanism | Countermeasure used below |
|---|---|---|
| UM DLL (`ACE-BASE.dll`, `SGuard`, `Tenio`, `TP` helper) | `LoadLibrary`/`LdrLoadDll` hooks, PEB `InLoadOrderModuleList` walk, `DbgBreakPoint`, `DbgUiRemoteBreakin`, `NtQueryInformationProcess(ProcessDebugPort/ObjectFlags)`, thread-context drift | **Zero user-mode injection.** All target data is read kernel-side via raw CR3 paging walk. No threads in game process. No handles to game process. |
| UM handle enumeration | `ObRegisterCallbacks` on `Obp*` / `ObTypeObjectType`, reports processes that hold handles to ACE modules, snapshots `SystemHandleInformation` | We hold **zero** handles to the target process. Physical read, not `MmCopyVirtualMemory`. |
| `\Device` / driver enumeration | `ObRegisterCallbacks` on device namespace, `PsLoadedModuleList` scan for unsigned drivers, `MmGetSystemRoutineAddress`, `FltRegisterFilter` list, `IopQuerySystemInformation(SystemModuleInformation)` | DKOM unlink from **all three** `LDR_DATA_TABLE_ENTRY` lists + `\Device` object removal + randomized pool tags + randomized driver filename/signature. |
| Nonpaged pool scanning | Signatures of known driver allocation patterns (used to find hidden/hijacked drivers) | Randomized4CC pool tag per build; no `MmAllocateNonPagedPoolNx` size classes that alias known tags. |
| Kernel integrity | SSDT / `Obp*` inline hook CRC, `PsLoadedModuleList` forward-pointer validation, `ObHead` sentinel checks | Unlink must fix **forward AND backward** links (poison-list trap). Implemented below with the full triple-link fixup. |
| Callback-object integrity | `ObRegisterCallbackObject` list and `ObpCallbackObject` lookup | We never register. We also scrub `KeServiceDescriptorTable`-adjacent state by never touching it. |
| Minifilter / FS callback | `FltFindFirstFilterInstance`, `FltGetFilterName` | We perform no file I/O post-load. Config travels in a `SECTION` mapped by a **name-less** device. |
| Timing/behavior | `QueryPerformanceCounter` per-frame variance, camera-rotation vs mouse-Δ entropy | Read happens in a passive `IoWorkerThread` at 1–4 kHz, decoupled from render; output is emitted as a *bounded* filter with human-plausible slew and acceleration limits (see §D). |

## B. Data path

```
 KMBox/Arduino (or usermode SendInput on a throwaway input desktop)
        ▲  HID report, 125 Hz
        │
 usermode client  ──reads──►  \Device\<8 random hex>  (device name = per-build secret,
        │ randomized at build time, not in any static string table)
        ▼
 kdrv.sys ── IoWorkerThread: sleep 1..4 ms ── loop
        ├─ CR3 (KeGetCurrentCr3 on System idle thread / self) 
        ├─ EPROCESS scan from PsInitialSystemProcess→ActiveProcessLinks (no handle, no Ob hooks)
        ├─ DirectoryTableBase → manual 4-level PML4 walk → PEB → PEB_LDR_DATA→InLoadOrderMemoryModuleList
        ├─ module base → read via physical translation (MmMapIoSpace / MDL over physical)
        ├─ CameraCachePrivate (UE4) → ViewLocation + Rotation
        ├─ UWorld→GameState→PlayerArray → actor → RootComponent→RelativeLocation
        ├─ selection: dFov/dt/visible/team/health
        └─ output: worldToScreen → target point → desired pitch/yaw
                    → slew limiter (critically-damped) → shared ring buffer
```

No kernel writes to game memory at all. Read-only. That is the single biggest decision: **write detection is fingerprintable via page-dirty / CR3 shadow diffs; read detection is only detectable by instrumenting the CR3 walker, which requires Pagefault-handler or EPT hooks that ACE does not deploy on your client OS build.**

